#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# jacobi_amplitude

#% text
# `jacobi_amplitude(u, m)` gives the amplitude `am(u | m)`, the `phi` with `elliptic_f(phi, m) = u`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The amplitude inverts the elliptic integral of the first kind (DLMF 22.16.1); `am(u | 0)` is `u`, `am(u | 1)` is the Gudermannian of `u`.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
jacobi_amplitude(1.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= 0.9323150798838539

#% cell
jacobi_amplitude(u, 0)
#% output
#= u

#% cell
jacobi_amplitude(2.0, 0.9)
#% output
#= 1.378750823589225

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `sin` of the amplitude is `jacobi_sn`:

#% cell
sin(jacobi_amplitude(0.7, 0.4)) - jacobi_sn(0.7, 0.4)
#% output
#= -1.1102230246251565e-16

#% text
# The inverse of `elliptic_f`:

#% cell
elliptic_f(jacobi_amplitude(1.3, 0.6), 0.6)
#% output
#= 1.3

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `elliptic_f`, `jacobi_sn`, `jacobi_cn`, `jacobi_dn`
